@startuml redis-02-lifecycle
!include _style.iuml
title Extension lifecycle — and why teardown is *not* in afterAll

|#0f141a|JUnit 5 Platform|
start
:discover test class;
:walk annotation graph\n(**meta-annotation search** — the JLS\ngives no annotation inheritance here);
:register **RedisContainerExtension**\nvia @ExtendWith on @RedisStandalone;

|#0f141a|beforeAll(context)|
:CURRENT_CONTEXT.set(context)\n<i>ThreadLocal — powers INSTANCE.get(id)</i>;
:extractAnnotations()\n<i>@RedisStandalones container, or single</i>;
:**ResourceBudget** validate\n<i>guard clause — throws BEFORE any Docker call</i>;
if (enableNetworkChaos / enableConnectionChaos?) then (yes)
  :DependencyVerifier.require…Module();
else (no)
endif
:**StandaloneStartupOrchestrator.start()**\nscatter–gather + bulkhead\n(fixed pool, 60 s per instance);
:Store.put(CloseableStoreMap)\n<i>wrapper exists because JUnit closes only</i>\n<i>directly-stored CloseableResource values</i>;

|#0f141a|test methods|
:ParameterResolver injects\nStandaloneRedis / List<StandaloneRedis>;
:...your assertions...;

|#0f141a|afterAll(context)|
:CURRENT_CONTEXT.remove();
note right #3a1c1c
  **That is all afterAll does.**
  It does *not* stop containers.
end note

|#0f141a|Store close (after afterAll)|
:CloseableStoreMap.close()\n→ Store::close → container.stop();
:Testcontainers **Ryuk** reaps anything left\n<i>second, independent cleanup path —</i>\n<i>survives kill -9 of the Gradle worker</i>;
stop
@enduml
